  <article class="prose page">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I'm Gian — a research engineer working on <strong>reinforcement learning for
      LLM agents</strong>. I research the methods, then ship the ones that survive
      contact with reality as a Python SDK: composable primitives for rewards,
      rollouts and evals, PyTorch-style, so training an agent feels like writing
      code rather than tuning prompts.
    </p>

    <p>
      Right now that means one training loop that any agent can plug into, GRPO-style
      policy optimisation for agents that learn from their own execution traces
      (benchmarked against prompt-optimisation approaches like GEPA's Pareto search),
      and an eval harness strict enough to decide what ships — improvement on
      held-out tasks, not on training reward.
    </p>

    <h2>Before this</h2>

    <p>
      I co-founded <strong>Unakin</strong> and was CTO there from 2023 to 2026. We
      built <strong>Sawyer</strong>, an autonomous coding agent that plans, writes
      and iterates on game code inside Unity with full project context — code,
      scenes and assets. I trained our custom Llama 2/3 models in PyTorch (full
      fine-tuning, extended pre-training, LoRA) on multi-stage pipelines across our
      own 8× A100 cluster, with FSDP, DDP and custom LoRA-aware sharding, and shipped
      multi-agent orchestration and real-time inference to enterprise customers.
      Along the way I fine-tuned text-conditioned diffusion models for 3D generation
      and made inference ~10× faster by swapping out the NeRF backbone at inference
      time. I built and led a 6-person engineering team, and raised a $1.5M pre-seed
      (Betaworks, Greycroft, Entrepreneur First, Mercuri).
    </p>

    <p>
      Much of my work lives at the boundary between models and the hardware they run
      on. One kernel I'm fond of — a custom Triton cross-entropy — cut memory roughly
      in half versus stock PyTorch and let us double the batch size, the kind of
      change that pays for itself every step of training.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before all this I was a physicist. I did my PhD in particle physics with the
      John Adams Institute (Oxford and Royal Holloway), based largely at CERN,
      leading development of Monte Carlo simulation software and distributed
      data-processing pipelines for high-energy physics experiments. I was a core
      contributor to
      <a href="https://github.com/jairhul/pyg4ometry">pyg4ometry</a>, an open-source
      geometry library still used across HEP. That background is where my instinct
      for numerical rigor and large-scale systems comes from.
    </p>

    <h2>Focus areas</h2>

